WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.467
A producer hands millions of messages a second to a consumer through a volatile field.

2
00:00:08.667 --> 00:00:11.971
Marking it volatile puts the rules on the field.

3
00:00:11.971 --> 00:00:17.341
Every write pays for the strongest rules: on x86, a drain of the store buffer.

4
00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:20.504
A VarHandle moves the rules onto each access.

5
00:00:20.504 --> 00:00:24.748
The field stays plain, and each read or write picks its own mode.

6
00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:26.765
Take one handoff.

7
00:00:26.765 --> 00:00:29.868
The writer sets total and count, then ready.

8
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.944
The reader waits for ready, then reads total.

9
00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:37.483
Plain mode promises nothing about what the other thread sees, or when.

10
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.564
The JIT may read ready once for the whole loop.

11
00:00:40.564 --> 00:00:42.087
The reader spins forever.

12
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.592
Opaque makes every access really happen, so the reader does see ready turn true.

13
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.477
But it orders nothing else.

14
00:00:49.477 --> 00:00:53.701
ready can arrive before total, and the reader reads a total of zero.

15
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:58.911
On ARM, jcstress caught this in 0.68 percent of samples.

16
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:01.724
Now make the last write a release.

17
00:01:01.724 --> 00:01:06.936
Nothing written before it may move after it, like a sealed envelope that holds all of it.

18
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:08.311
The release lands.

19
00:01:08.311 --> 00:01:11.743
The reader reads ready with an acquire, and sees true.

20
00:01:11.743 --> 00:01:13.459
What total can it read now?

21
00:01:14.967 --> 00:01:16.158
Only 1,250,000.

22
00:01:16.158 --> 00:01:23.065
Everything the writer did before the release is visible after the acquire that sees it.

23
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:32.037
Release and acquire still allow one odd result: each thread writes its own flag, reads the other's, and both read zero.

24
00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:34.142
Volatile forbids it.

25
00:01:34.142 --> 00:01:40.773
All threads agree on one order of every volatile access, so at least one of them sees the other's write.

26
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.319
So the modes form a ladder.

27
00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:46.947
Each one keeps everything below it, and adds one promise.

28
00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:52.326
On x86, only the volatile write costs extra: it drains the store buffer.

29
00:01:52.326 --> 00:01:54.476
So a release write is cheaper.

30
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.640
In code, READY is a VarHandle for the field ready.

31
00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.511
The writer calls setRelease.

32
00:02:00.511 --> 00:02:02.381
The reader calls getAcquire.

33
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:03.785
The rule.

34
00:02:03.785 --> 00:02:11.064
A release write hands over everything written before it, to the acquire read that sees it, on the same variable.

35
00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:12.306
Check yourself.

36
00:02:12.306 --> 00:02:17.293
A volatile field is read in a hot loop, with a plain get on a VarHandle.

37
00:02:17.293 --> 00:02:18.817
Is that read volatile?

38
00:02:21.533 --> 00:02:21.736
No.

39
00:02:21.736 --> 00:02:23.624
The mode at the access wins.

40
00:02:23.624 --> 00:02:28.209
It is a plain read, and the JIT may read it once for the whole loop.
